---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A switch board fidget is a grid of latching toggles you flip in the browser. It is free, needs
      no download, and runs in the tab with no app and no account. Open the page, tap a switch, hear
      an optional click, and keep going. When the board is a mess of mixed latches, hit All off and
      start again.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example: Input: a phone-width tab and sixteen switches in four columns. Output: a quiet
      sensory loop with optional sound and haptics, no install, and nothing uploaded about which
      switches you flipped.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore this page is a toggle switch board you can use on a call with sound muted, or on a
      phone with haptics on, without granting notification permissions.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Switch Board →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-is">
    <h2>What is a switch board fidget? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      People search for satisfying switches and mechanical switch simulators when they want the
      click of a real toggle without a hardware board on the desk. A video of someone flipping
      switches is the usual result. It looks right and does nothing when you tap it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page is the interactive version: sixteen latches in four columns, sized for a phone-width screen.
      Each switch stays on or off until you flip it again. That is the whole toy. There is no score,
      no timer, and no skin shop.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, the useful part is control. Sound and vibration are separate. Keyboard users get one
      tab stop and arrow keys, not sixteen stops. All off continues the loop without a reload.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How does this switch board online work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The board opens with every switch off. Tap one and three things happen in order.
    </p>
    <p>
      The switch marks itself latched so a second event on the same press cannot fire again.
      The Feel engine plays a short click if Sounds are on.
      If Haptics are opted in, the same call asks the phone to vibrate once, and a missing
      vibrator is ignored instead of throwing.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, on a laptop with haptics left off, you still see the switch depress and may hear
      the click. On a phone with haptics on, you get the buzz as well. Sound and vibration stay
      independent, therefore muting for an open office does not turn vibration off for later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reduced motion (from your system or Settings → Feel) turns off switch transitions. Taps still
      latch and All off still works. Once Feel loads, the Full or Reduced choice under
      Settings → Feel overrides the system preference.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sound">
    <h2>How do online switch fidgets handle sound? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Badly, on many pages. Always-on audio is fine alone and unusable on a shared desk. Phone apps
      often bury mute behind an IAP skin, or they tie sound and vibration to one switch so muting
      also kills haptics.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here, both channels go through <code>ToyTools.feel</code>. The click is a short tone the
      browser synthesises. There is no audio file to download. Sounds default on; haptics default
      off. Change either under the board or Settings → Feel. A rejected haptic promise never stops
      the latch.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore you can mute switch clicks but keep haptics for a phone session, or the reverse on
      a quiet call. That is how to use a switch board online without installing an app and without
      fighting the page for control of your speakers.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Leaving sound on in a shared space">
      <p>
        Blaming the page for every click ignores the Settings → Feel toggle. Mute once and every
        fidget on the site stays quiet, including this one.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Expecting desktop vibration">
      <p>
        Most laptops have no vibrator. Haptics stay off by default, and even when opted in a missing
        API is ignored so the switch still latches.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="all-off">
    <h2>How to clear a mixed switch board <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      After flipping around, the board is a mess of mixed latches. Tap All off. That inflates
      nothing and scores nothing: it turns every switch off, leaves Feel preferences alone, and
      stays hidden while the board is already clear.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reloading the tab when All off would continue the session is the common mistake this control
      exists to remove. Refreshing works, but it also drops focus and reloads assets.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Reload is not All off">
      <p>
        A mixed board looks like a dead end. All off is the continuation: every latch ready again,
        without leaving the tool.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>
      Another failure shows up as a toggle that animates then snaps back: press and release both
      fire. This board latches on pointerdown and ignores the matching click, so the paired event
      is a no-op.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="when-it-helps">
    <h2>When a browser switch board helps <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Quietly flipping switches through a long call with sound muted is the usual case. How to use
      a switch board with a keyboard is the same grid: Tab to the board, arrows to move, Space to
      flip. Giving someone a clicky fidget without ads or accounts is a third, because the page is
      just buttons.
    </p>
    <p>
      Instead of an ASMR switch video, keep this tab beside a
      <a href={withBase('/tool/fidget/pop-it/')}>Pop It</a> when you want bubbles instead of latches,
      or beside a
      <a href={withBase('/tool/productivity/pomodoro-timer/')}>Pomodoro timer</a> between focus
      blocks. More toys live in the
      <a href={withBase('/category/fidgets/')}>Fidgets</a> category.
    </p>
    <p>
      Switch board vs alternatives is mostly about whether you can actually flip something. An ASMR
      video is not interactive. A phone simulator app wants permissions and IAP skins. This page
      asks for a tap. Pick the app only if you need a home screen icon. Otherwise the online board
      is enough. Once the tab has loaded, it keeps working without a server.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>Try the board <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Open the tool, flip a few switches, mute sound from the board toggles if you need to, and tap
      All off when the latches are a mess. That is the whole product.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Switch Board →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
